From: X6100 firmware project
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: btusb: keep events ahead of ACL data (poll sync on)

USB carries HCI events on the interrupt endpoint and ACL data on the bulk
endpoint, so the host can see them in a different order than the
controller sent them. On the Xiegu X6100 (RTL8723BU, Bluetooth and WiFi
on one USB device, sun8i musb host) this breaks LE pairing: the
peripheral's SMP key distribution arrives before the HCI Encryption
Change event, smp.c drops it ("unexpected SMP command 0x06 from ...")
and the pairing times out. btmon of a failed pairing with a BLE
keyboard (legacy Just Works, bonding):

  < LE Start Encryption
  > ACL  SMP: Encryption Information (0x06)     01.877710
  > HCI  Encryption Change: enabled             01.878009
  > ACL  SMP: Central Identification (0x07)     01.878133
  > ACL  SMP: Identity Information (0x08)
  > ACL  SMP: Identity Address Information (0x09)
  kernel: Bluetooth: hci0: unexpected SMP command 0x06 ... 0x09

It fails or works at random, more often while WiFi is busy on the same
USB device. btusb already has the remedy, force_poll_sync (commit
800fe5ec302e "Bluetooth: btusb: Add support for queuing during polling
interval"), off by default and switchable only while the adapter is
down, which on this system means a power cycle after boot. So:

- poll_sync is on from probe (still switchable in debugfs);
- the ACL delay is one jiffy longer than the interval: a delay of one
  jiffy may expire at the very next tick, and with HZ=100 that left
  the data held for anything from 0 to 10 ms, not one interval;
- btusb_recv_event() hands the event to the core before it wakes the
  worker that releases held ACL data, so that data can no longer get
  ahead of the event (as in "Bluetooth: btusb: Preserve event and ACL
  packet ordering", bluetooth-next 2026).

With force_poll_sync on, the same keyboard paired and bonded first time
(Encryption Change, then all four keys 13 ms later) and types. Incoming
ACL data waits 10-20 ms longer; SCO/ISO audio is not affected.

---
--- a/drivers/bluetooth/btusb.c
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/btusb.c
@@ -929,12 +929,20 @@
 
 static int btusb_recv_event(struct btusb_data *data, struct sk_buff *skb)
 {
+	int err;
+
+	/* Hand the event to the core first, then release the ACL data held
+	 * back: the other way round the worker can deliver that data ahead
+	 * of the event it was waiting for.
+	 */
+	err = data->recv_event(data->hdev, skb);
+
 	if (data->intr_interval) {
 		/* Trigger dequeue immediatelly if an event is received */
 		schedule_delayed_work(&data->rx_work, 0);
 	}
 
-	return data->recv_event(data->hdev, skb);
+	return err;
 }
 
 static int btusb_recv_intr(struct btusb_data *data, void *buffer, int count)
@@ -1002,7 +1010,11 @@
 		return data->recv_acl(data->hdev, skb);
 
 	skb_queue_tail(&data->acl_q, skb);
-	schedule_delayed_work(&data->rx_work, data->intr_interval);
+	/* One jiffy more than the interval: a delay of N jiffies may expire
+	 * at the very next tick, so this holds the data at least one whole
+	 * interval (with HZ=100 and a 1 ms interval: 10 to 20 ms, not 0).
+	 */
+	schedule_delayed_work(&data->rx_work, data->intr_interval + 1);
 
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -3844,6 +3856,13 @@
 
 	skb_queue_head_init(&data->acl_q);
 
+	/* Events (interrupt endpoint) and ACL data (bulk endpoint) can reach
+	 * the host out of order. force_poll_sync holds ACL data back for an
+	 * interrupt interval so that an event sent before it is handled
+	 * first. On by default here; still switchable in debugfs.
+	 */
+	data->poll_sync = true;
+
 	init_usb_anchor(&data->deferred);
 	init_usb_anchor(&data->tx_anchor);
 	spin_lock_init(&data->txlock);
